      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Subdural haematoma (SDH) is rare following spinal anaesthesia and has not been reported previously in an infant. Non-accidental injury is the commonest cause of subdural haematoma in infants."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We describe two cases of SDH following spinal anaesthesia in infants."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In both cases, forensic investigation was commenced and no evidence of child abuse was found. Both children are well 2 years after diagnosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Paediatric health workers should be aware of the possibility of SDH after spinal anaesthesia and consider this as a differential diagnosis when investigating possible non-accidental injury in an infant."
        }
      ]},
